  XsSolEcl: Q([['During a total solar eclipse, what can you see around the dark Moon?', 'The Sun\'s glowing corona', 'Earth\'s shadow|Clouds|Mars', 'The corona is the Sun\'s outer atmosphere.'], ['At which Moon phase can a solar eclipse happen?', 'New moon', 'Full moon|First quarter|Waxing gibbous', 'The Moon is between Earth and Sun.'], ['Why don\'t solar eclipses happen every month?', 'The Moon\'s orbit is tilted, so it usually passes above or below the Sun', 'The Moon stops|The Sun hides|Earth stops spinning', 'Alignment is rare.'], ['Why can a solar eclipse only be seen from part of Earth?', 'The Moon\'s shadow is small compared with Earth', 'The Sun is small|Eclipses are secret|Earth is flat', 'The shadow covers a narrow path.'], ['How can you safely watch a solar eclipse?', 'With certified eclipse glasses or a pinhole projector', 'With sunglasses|With binoculars alone|By staring briefly', 'Normal sunglasses are not safe.'], ['During a solar eclipse, what blocks the Sun\'s light?', 'The Moon', 'Earth|A cloud|Venus', 'The Moon passes in front of the Sun.'], ['How long does totality (complete darkness) usually last?', 'A few minutes', 'A whole day|A week|One second', 'Totality is brief.']]),
